On HEAD responses, `Close()` no longer closes the encoder when nothing was written, so the 20-byte gzip / 13-byte zstd empty-stream trailer stops reaching the client writer. That means net/http should no longer set a `Content-Length` from those bytes. I haven't run this or added a test.

`Close()` still sets the encoding headers in that case; it just returns the encoder to the pool without closing it. The change is limited to the path in `Close()` where no header or body was written yet, which is the HEAD case. SSE and incremental responses that set headers through `WriteHeader` still close normally. So an empty-bodied GET of that kind still gets a valid empty compressed stream.

